From 557224b70cbc236909b4211861154af6f7294da8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zjw <zhaojw@jwele.com.cn>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 10:47:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Library: drop the soft float dependency from TIMER_Delay

TIMER_Delay() formed u32Usec * u32Clk in float only to dodge a 32-bit
overflow (1e6 usec at 24 MHz is 2.4e13, which needs 45 bits).  On this
FPU-less Cortex-M0 that one expression is what pulls the entire soft
float library into the image: timer.o(.text.TIMER_Delay) is the sole
referrer to __aeabi_ui2f, __aeabi_fmul, __aeabi_fdiv and __aeabi_f2uiz,
and fepilogue.o is pulled only by fdiv.o and ffltui.o.  Rewriting it
reclaims the whole chain, 430 bytes:

    fdiv 124 + fepilogue 130 + ffixui 40 + ffltui 14 + fmul 122

Splitting both operands at 1000 keeps every intermediate inside 32 bits,
so this needs neither float nor a 64-bit multiply helper.  uint64_t is
deliberately avoided: Cortex-M0 is ARMv6-M and has no UMULL, so the
product would risk pulling in __aeabi_lmul, which is not in the image
today.  (A 64-bit *division* would be free - uldiv.o is already linked
via printf - but a 64-bit multiply is not.)

    a*b = (1000*a1 + a0) * (1000*b1 + b0)
        = 1e6*a1*b1 + 1000*s + t     s = a1*b0 + a0*b1
                                     t = a0*b0

so  a*b/1e6 = a1*b1 + (1000*s + t)/1e6, and with s = 1000*q + r that
second term is q + ((1000*r + t) >= 1e6), because 1000*r + t < 2e6.  That
carry is the last term of the new expression, which makes it the exact
quotient rather than an approximation.

Behaviour is unchanged and in fact slightly better.  The float version was
not exact to begin with - 24 mantissa bits cannot resolve 1.2e12 any finer
than 131072, and over a 20M-sample sweep of the documented domain it
disagreed with the exact quotient about 22% of the time; it merely happened
to be exact at this firmware's 12 MHz operating point.  The new expression
is exact everywhere, and at 12 MHz produces a bit-identical compare value
for every delay this project uses (10/100/500/1000/10000/100000 usec).

Bounds: u32Clk is at most 0xFFFFFF at this point (the prescale branch just
above), and TIMER_Delay is documented for u32Usec up to 1e6, so a1 <= 1000,
b1 <= 16777, s <= 1.78e7 and 1000*r + t <= 2.0e6 - all far inside 32 bits,
and the quotient stays well inside the 24-bit TCMPR.

Verified on the host by extracting the changed lines verbatim and
comparing against 64-bit arithmetic: zero mismatches over the full
documented domain.  A Keil rebuild is still needed to confirm the byte
saving, since no ARM toolchain is available here.

Note this is the first change to a vendor file under Library/ in this
repository; the two commits that have ever touched Library/ are both build
setup.

This file is CRLF, so apply it with either

    git apply size-reduction-timer-delay-softfloat.patch

or

    git am --keep-cr size-reduction-timer-delay-softfloat.patch

The --keep-cr matters: plain `git am` strips the trailing CR from each
line while splitting the mail, and the patch then no longer matches a
CRLF source file.  Both of the above were tested to reproduce the working
tree byte for byte.

---
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Library/StdDriver/src/timer.c b/Library/StdDriver/src/timer.c
index 557224b..767483e 100755
--- a/Library/StdDriver/src/timer.c
+++ b/Library/StdDriver/src/timer.c
@@ -84,7 +84,6 @@ void TIMER_Delay(TIMER_T *timer, uint32_t u32Usec)
 {
     uint32_t u32Clk = TIMER_GetModuleClock(timer);
     uint32_t u32Prescale = 0, delay = SystemCoreClock / u32Clk;
-    float fCmpr;
 
     // Clear current timer configuration
     timer->TCSR = 0;
@@ -101,10 +100,40 @@ void TIMER_Delay(TIMER_T *timer, uint32_t u32Usec)
         u32Clk >>= 1;
     }
 
-    // u32Usec * u32Clk might overflow if using uint32_t
-    fCmpr = ((float)u32Usec * (float)u32Clk) / 1000000.0;
-
-    timer->TCMPR = (uint32_t)fCmpr;
+    // u32Usec * u32Clk might overflow if using uint32_t : up to 1e6 usec at
+    // 24 MHz is 2.4e13, which needs 45 bits.  The original code formed the
+    // product in float, and on this FPU-less Cortex-M0 that one line pulled
+    // the whole soft float library into the image - fdiv, fmul, ffixui,
+    // ffltui and fepilogue, 430 bytes - to compute what only ever becomes a
+    // 24-bit compare value.
+    //
+    // Splitting both operands at 1000 keeps every step inside 32 bits, so
+    // this needs neither float nor a 64-bit multiply helper (the M0 has no
+    // UMULL, so forming the product in uint64_t would pull in __aeabi_lmul) :
+    //
+    //     a*b = (1000*a1 + a0) * (1000*b1 + b0)
+    //         = 1e6*a1*b1 + 1000*s + t     s = a1*b0 + a0*b1
+    //                                      t = a0*b0
+    //
+    // so  a*b/1e6 = a1*b1 + (1000*s + t)/1e6, and with s = 1000*q + r that
+    // second term is q + ((1000*r + t) >= 1e6), since 1000*r + t < 2e6.  The
+    // last line below is that carry.  So this is the exact quotient, not an
+    // approximation - the float version could not even be exact, carrying
+    // only 24 mantissa bits, which at the 1.2e12 of a 100ms delay at 12MHz
+    // resolve no finer than 131072.
+    //
+    // Bounds : u32Clk is at most 0xFFFFFF here (the prescale branch above)
+    // and TIMER_Delay is documented for u32Usec up to 1e6, so a1 <= 1000,
+    // b1 <= 16777, s <= 1.78e7 and 1000*r + t <= 2.0e6 - all far inside
+    // 32 bits, and the quotient stays well inside the 24-bit TCMPR.
+    {
+        uint32_t a1 = u32Usec / 1000, a0 = u32Usec % 1000;
+        uint32_t b1 = u32Clk  / 1000, b0 = u32Clk  % 1000;
+        uint32_t s  = a1 * b0 + a0 * b1;
+
+        timer->TCMPR = a1 * b1 + s / 1000
+                     + (((s % 1000) * 1000 + a0 * b0) >= 1000000u);
+    }
     timer->TCSR = TIMER_TCSR_CEN_Msk | u32Prescale; // one shot mode
 
     // When system clock is faster than timer clock, it is possible timer active bit cannot set in time while we check it.

-- 
